# Shipped dist-assets are generated by `ziv` on macOS/Linux and compared byte-for-byte against
# what the binary emits (`crates/cli/tests/dist_assets_are_current.rs`). `actions/checkout` does
# not override Git for Windows' `core.autocrlf`, so without this a Windows CI runner checks these
# out with CRLF and the drift guard fails there even though nothing is actually stale.
dist-assets/** text eol=lf

# Test fixtures are Zarr stores: byte-exact data, not text. Their chunk files are raw binary, and
# their JSON metadata is compared byte-for-byte against the generator by the fixture drift checks
# (crates/fixture-test-support). Git for Windows' core.autocrlf rewrote that JSON to CRLF on
# checkout (a 339-byte .zattrs arrived as 352 bytes), so every drift check failed on Windows only.
# `-text` rather than `text eol=lf`: forcing text handling onto the binary chunks would let git
# normalise bytes inside pixel data. Nothing under tests/fixtures/ is ever converted.
tests/fixtures/** -text
